From 711cc82435692f601dd214233210b2fe9eaa67a1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 20:55:09 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 09959/11362] [outer-loop] Preserve in-flight state before an
 operator reboot

The operator is shutting down to recover an RTX 3090 that fell off the driver at
2026-08-25 18:39Z. nvidia-smi has reported one device since; the kernel has logged
an nvidia-modeset progress error every five seconds for six hours.

Committing rather than stashing, per the standing never-revert rule. Contents are
the conductor's own in-flight work: 44 exp6614 prospective-invariant checkpoints
whose only changed field is `predictions`, plus conductor state and log files. No
determination keys were dropped, verified by field-level diff against HEAD before
staging.

State at shutdown:
- Milestone 2026.08.576, 9 artifacts: 3 terminal, 6 blocked, 0 flagged. Two of the
  blocks are the VRAM squeeze that preceded the card loss.
- The conductor's generator, pid 675677, has held no device memory since the card
  went away. It is stopped with everything else.
- The shadow-versus-applied supervisor experiment COMPLETED before shutdown, 6 of 6
  cells in both arms. Result recorded separately: zero redirects fired in all twelve
  cells across roughly 4,700 actions, so observe() behaves identically in shadow and
  applied mode and the stagnation detector never fires on these games at this budget.

A conductor-hold marker is set at ~/.carnot/conductor-hold so the janitor does not
restart the conductor during the outage. REMOVE IT after the reboot or the conductor
stays down.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01JntPrUGLNesPcJH1pyfFYw
